      "title": "Lesson 5 | Linguistic and termed definition of Ḍabṭ",
      "overview": "This lesson explains how ḥadīth scholars determine whether a narrator is reliable, focusing on two foundational conditions: al-ʿAdālah (ٱلْعَدَالَة) and al-Ḍabṭ (ٱلضَّبْط). ʿAdālah refers to the narrator’s moral uprightness, religious commitment, and honesty, ensuring that he would not intentionally lie about the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. Scholars judge this based on outward behavior (al-ẓāhir) rather than hidden realities. However, since mistakes can occur even with good intentions, a second condition is required: ḍabṭ, which means precision in preserving and transmitting what was heard. This protects the Sunnah not from deliberate fabrication, but from human error such as forgetfulness, confusion, or inattention.\n\nThe lesson further explains that ḍabṭ has two recognized forms: Ḍabṭ al-Ṣadr (ضبط الصدر), precision through strong memorization, and Ḍabṭ al-Kitāb (ضبط الكتاب), precision through accurate written preservation. Most scholars relied heavily on written records, though exceptional memorization did exist. The discussion also clarifies that deep legal understanding is not a condition for narrator reliability, as preserving wording differs from deriving rulings. Additionally, it introduces the concept of Riwayah bi-l-Maʿnā (الرواية بالمعنى)—narrating by meaning—which is permitted by many scholars under strict conditions to avoid altering intended meanings, especially in concise, eloquent prophetic statements. Together, ʿadālah and ḍabṭ form the core framework through which the authenticity of ḥadīth transmission is critically evaluated.",
